<section class="manifesto"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#7ad5c7">A different question</div><div class="row" style="margin-top:16px"><div><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#8ea3ba">A conventional process-level question</div><p class="q">What does the process usually look like?</p></div><div><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#7ad5c7">RE-ViVE’s execution-level question</div><p class="q">What happened to this loan, this payment, this customer, and why?</p></div></div><p class="after">The first question is answered with a picture. The second needs evidence. Banks are judged on the second: by customers, by examiners, and by their own boards. <b>RE-ViVE is built to answer it.</b></p></div></section><section class="section" id="philosophy" style="background:#eef6f8"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head" style="max-width:950px"><div class="eyebrow">The RE-ViVE design philosophy</div><h2>Don't infer the process.<br/><span class="accent">Reconstruct the execution.</span></h2><p class="lead" style="max-width:900px">RE-ViVE deterministically reconstructs execution from operational events and their relationships, rather than starting with an assumed process model and trying to fit behavior around it.</p><p class="quote" style="border-left:5px solid #167f79;padding-left:24px;margin-top:26px">The process map is the result of that reconstruction — not the assumption that drives it.</p><p>That difference makes individual paths, independent activities, exceptions, changing structures and source-level evidence central to the analysis, not afterthoughts.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="architecture"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow">A different starting point</div><h2>The maps look alike.<br/><span class="accent">The foundations don't.</span></h2><p>Process mining offers many discovery and modeling techniques. RE-ViVE takes a different starting point: operational events and their relationships are used to reconstruct each observed execution deterministically. The resulting maps can be analyzed without relying on a predefined ideal process or a statistically inferred representative path.</p></div><div class="compare"><div><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#6c6588">Discovery-first</div><div class="stack"><div class="step">Event log and a chosen case perspective</div><div class="arrow">↓</div><div class="step">Discover, abstract and simplify a process model</div><div class="arrow">↓</div><div class="step">Explain the paths and exceptions around it</div></div><p class="mini">In some discovery-centered views, aggregation, filtering and the choice of case perspective can obscure low-frequency behavior or cross-object relationships.</p></div><div><div class="eyebrow">Execution-first (RE-ViVE)</div><div class="stack"><div class="step">Operational events, identities and relationships</div><div class="arrow">↓</div><div class="step">Deterministic reconstruction of each execution</div><div class="arrow">↓</div><div class="step">Paths, exceptions and context you can verify</div></div><p class="mini">No predefined ideal process. No statistically representative path. The map is a result, not an assumption.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section alt" id="proof"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow">Head-to-head, in a bank</div><h2>Same bank. Same data.<br/><span class="accent">100+ workflows against zero.</span></h2><p>In a banking evaluation reported by RE-ViVE, our team reconstructed more than 100 workflows from operational data. According to the evaluation account, another approach received comparable or more extensive data but had not delivered a completed workflow after approximately three months.</p></div><div class="proofrow"><div class="card" style="border-top:5px solid #167f79"><div class="eyebrow">RE-ViVE</div><div class="proof">100+</div><h3>Workflows reconstructed</h3><p>Real operational data, many banking workflows, each verifiable at the source.</p></div><div class="card" style="border-top:5px solid #a9b5c5"><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#69798d">Alternative approach</div><div class="proof">0</div><h3>Completed at the deadline</h3><p>About three months in, on comparable or more extensive data.</p></div><div class="card challenge"><div class="eyebrow">Run it yourself</div><h3>Same data. Same scope. Same clock.</h3><p>We'll take a side-by-side on your workflows. Agree the data, the success criteria and the time window up front, then compare completeness, traceability, accuracy and time to a useful answer.</p></div></div><p class="footnote" style="margin-top:18px">Evaluation reported by RE-ViVE; the bank is not named.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow">Why this is hard to copy</div><h2>Four persistent challenges.<br/><span class="accent">One different starting point.</span></h2><p>Researchers continue to improve how process analysis represents concurrency, multidimensional context and evolving relationships. RE-ViVE addresses these needs through its deterministic execution foundation, rather than requiring the same discovery-and-abstraction assumptions.</p></div><div class="grid g2 moat"><article class="card"><div class="num">01 / CONCURRENCY</div><h3>Parallel work is not rework.</h3><p>A directly-follows graph can suggest loops when independent activities finish in different orders. RE-ViVE identifies same-level activities and distinguishes parallel work from actual repetition.</p><span class="eg">For example: sanctions screening and ID verification can finish in either order without either check being repeated.</span></article><article class="card"><div class="num">02 / CONTEXT</div><h3>30–100 dimensions. One execution foundation.</h3><p>RE-ViVE implementations support dozens of process-context dimensions, not just filters on time or amount. Teams can examine relationships across product, channel, branch, policy, risk and other business perspectives without separately rebuilding the underlying execution for each view.</p><span class="eg">For example: Product, channel, branch, risk tier, relationship manager, policy version: same data, any view.</span></article><article class="card"><div class="num">03 / CHANGE</div><h3>When the workflow changes, the view does too.</h3><p>RE-ViVE captures changing relationships and reconstructs the execution of each period as observed. New activities, retired steps and altered routes can be compared over time without forcing them into a fixed process template.</p><span class="eg">For example: A new fraud check added in Q2 shows up in Q2, not as noise across the year.</span></article><article class="card"><div class="num">04 / AI AGENTS</div><h3>Agents tied to business outcomes.</h3><p>The same reconstruction extends to AI agents, tool calls and traces. Where business keys and telemetry exist, agent behaviour links to the loans, cases and payments it touched.</p><span class="eg">For example: An extraction agent's retries traced to the disputes that missed their deadline.</span></article></div><div style="margin-top:28px;border:1px solid #c9e0e4;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#eff9f7,#f1f5fc);padding:26px;border-radius:18px"><div class="eyebrow">One foundation · multiple perspectives</div><h3 style="margin:9px 0 5px">The evidence stays the same. The question changes.</h3><p style="max-width:850px">Parallel activities are recognized as peers, context dimensions expose different populations, and period comparisons reveal new or retired steps. None requires inventing a representative execution.</p><div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:22px"><div class="card"><div class="num">PARALLEL EXECUTION</div><svg aria-label="One start splits into parallel KYC and credit checks before joining for approval" role="img" viewbox="0 0 270 125"><g fill="none" stroke="#368c87" stroke-width="3"><path d="M24 63 H65 L91 31 H172 L198 63 H244"></path><path d="M65 63 L91 96 H172 L198 63"></path></g><g fill="#fff" stroke="#167f79" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="24" cy="63" r="8"></circle><circle cx="244" cy="63" r="8"></circle><rect height="28" rx="7" width="83" x="90" y="17"></rect><rect height="28" rx="7" width="83" x="90" y="82"></rect></g><g fill="#23465d" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="131" y="35">KYC</text><text x="131" y="100">Credit</text><text x="24" y="82">Start</text><text x="244" y="82">Approve</text></g></svg><p class="mini">Independent checks, not a false rework loop.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="num">BUSINESS CONTEXT</div><svg aria-label="One common execution line viewed through three business dimensions" role="img" viewbox="0 0 270 125"><path d="M20 60 H250" stroke="#b3c8d8" stroke-width="4"></path><g fill="#167f79"><circle cx="38" cy="60" r="9"></circle><circle cx="105" cy="60" r="9"></circle><circle cx="172" cy="60" r="9"></circle><circle cx="239" cy="60" r="9"></circle></g><g font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><rect fill="#e6f3ef" height="26" rx="13" width="77" x="8" y="6"></rect><text fill="#167f79" x="46" y="23">Product</text><rect fill="#e9effa" height="26" rx="13" width="77" x="96" y="6"></rect><text fill="#3276b4" x="134" y="23">Channel</text><rect fill="#efe9f9" height="26" rx="13" width="77" x="184" y="6"></rect><text fill="#7653a7" x="222" y="23">Risk tier</text></g><path d="M46 32 V48 M134 32 V48 M222 32 V48" stroke="#9eb8c9" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></path></svg><p class="mini">Different analytical perspectives on the same observed executions.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="num">CHANGE OVER TIME</div><svg aria-label="Earlier period has three activities and later period adds a fraud review" role="img" viewbox="0 0 270 125"><g fill="none" stroke="#b3c8d8" stroke-width="3"><path d="M25 37 H245"></path><path d="M25 91 H245"></path></g><g fill="#167f79"><circle cx="35" cy="37" r="8"></circle><circle cx="135" cy="37" r="8"></circle><circle cx="235" cy="37" r="8"></circle><circle cx="35" cy="91" r="8"></circle><circle cx="105" cy="91" r="8"></circle><circle cx="235" cy="91" r="8"></circle></g><circle cx="172" cy="91" fill="#db9c45" r="9"></circle><g fill="#284963" font-size="10" font-weight="700"><text x="10" y="17">Q1</text><text x="10" y="72">Q2</text><text x="149" y="114">New fraud check</text></g></svg><p class="mini">New steps emerge as the operational process changes.</p></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section navy"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#7ad5c7">Evidence, not approximation</div><h2>Every path counted.<br/><span class="accent">Every exception kept.</span></h2><p>Rare paths are where the risk, the cost and the customer complaints live. RE-ViVE keeps the whole in-scope population and shows exactly what was and wasn't accounted for.</p></div><div class="grid g3"><div class="card dark"><h3>Deterministic</h3><p>Paths come from identifiable relationships in the records, not from a statistically representative sample.</p></div><div class="card dark"><h3>Reconciled</h3><p>The source population is checked against the result: what was represented, what was unmatched, what evidence was incomplete.</p></div><div class="card dark"><h3>Drill to the record</h3><p>Go from a pattern to a single request and its activity instances, then open the source system and confirm it.</p></div></div><div style="margin-top:30px;padding:24px;border:1px solid #43617f;border-radius:16px;background:#1b3a5b"><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#a9dfd8">Population reconciliation · illustrative</div><div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:15px"><div><div class="stat">100,000</div><div style="color:#cbdce9">In-scope transactions</div></div><div><div class="stat">100,000</div><div style="color:#cbdce9">Accounted for</div></div></div><div class="metric-band"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><div class="legend" style="color:#dce9f3"><span><b style="background:#178377"></b>72% standard</span><span><b style="background:#427cb8"></b>17% rework</span><span><b style="background:#7657b7"></b>8% exception</span><span><b style="background:#d08b43"></b>3% other</span></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="evidence"><div class="wrap evidence"><div><div class="eyebrow">Built for scrutiny</div><h2>When the examiner asks,<br/><span class="accent">open the record.</span></h2><p class="lead">Banking teams have traced individual transactions and requests surfaced by RE-ViVE back to their underlying operational systems to verify what actually happened.</p><p>Whether the record originates in loan origination, core banking, payment processing, or a case-management platform, the investigation can move from the execution insight to the relevant source evidence.</p><p>That matters wherever a workflow touches customers, controls, service levels or regulatory obligations. A finding you can trace to the source is a finding you can act on, defend, and fix.</p><div class="pill">From the insight back to the source</div></div><div class="svg-panel"><svg aria-label="Banking transaction and execution linked to records in loan origination, core banking, and payment or case management systems" role="img" viewbox="0 0 500 285"><rect fill="#f3f8fc" height="285" rx="16" width="500"></rect><g fill="white" stroke="#cbdde8" stroke-width="1.5"><rect height="64" rx="10" width="144" x="19" y="27"></rect><rect height="64" rx="10" width="144" x="185" y="27"></rect><rect height="64" rx="10" width="130" x="351" y="27"></rect></g><g fill="#24445d" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="91" y="49">Loan 10842</text><text font-size="10" font-weight="400" x="91" y="69">Business transaction</text><text x="257" y="49">Execution path</text><text font-size="10" font-weight="400" x="257" y="69">6 observed steps</text><text x="416" y="49">Exception</text><text font-size="10" font-weight="400" x="416" y="69">Income re-verified</text></g><path d="M91 91 V129 H250 M257 91 V129 M416 91 V129 H257" fill="none" stroke="#5a95a6" stroke-width="2.5"></path><rect fill="#1c4b68" height="49" rx="11" width="280" x="110" y="129"></rect><text fill="white" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" x="250" y="149">RECONSTRUCTED EXECUTION</text><text fill="#c2e7e5" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" x="250" y="166">Every step traceable to operational evidence</text><path d="M250 178 V206 M75 206 H425 M75 206 V224 M250 206 V224 M425 206 V224" fill="none" stroke="#6b9cac" stroke-width="2"></path><g fill="white" stroke="#a6c7d4"><rect height="40" rx="8" width="126" x="12" y="224"></rect><rect height="40" rx="8" width="126" x="187" y="224"></rect><rect height="40" rx="8" width="126" x="362" y="224"></rect></g><g fill="#294d65" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="75" y="242">Loan origination</text><text font-size="9" font-weight="400" x="75" y="254">Decision event</text><text x="250" y="242">Core banking</text><text font-size="9" font-weight="400" x="250" y="254">Funding posting</text><text x="425" y="242">Payments / cases</text><text font-size="9" font-weight="400" x="425" y="254">Review event</text></g></svg></div></div></section>
<section class="section alt" id="ai"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow">Where this goes next</div><h2>Agents are entering these workflows.<br/><span class="accent">Someone has to see what they do.</span></h2><p>Loan files, disputes, onboarding and payment exceptions are already being handed, step by step, to AI agents working beside people. A dashboard of agent metrics can't tell you whether the loan closed on time. RE-ViVE connects the two: the business execution and the agent execution, on one reconstructed timeline.</p></div><div class="grid g3"><div class="card"><div class="num">HISTORICAL</div><h3>Reconstruct and compare</h3><p>How work ran, how paths and structure changed, and which exceptions are new.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="num">CONTINUOUS</div><h3>Watch as events arrive</h3><p>Refreshed or real-time views, as fast as source data and deployment allow.</p></div><div class="card" style="border-top:5px solid #7653a7"><div class="num" style="color:#7653a7">AI EXECUTION INTELLIGENCE</div><h3>People, agents and systems</h3><p>Business steps linked to agent runs, tool calls, retries and traces, wherever telemetry and business keys connect them.</p></div></div><p style="margin-top:30px;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy)">Built in banking. Not limited to it.</p><div class="reach" style="margin-top:10px"><span class="on">Banking</span><span>Capital markets</span><span>Insurance</span><span>Healthcare</span></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="research"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="eyebrow">Further reading</div><h2>The problems are documented.<br/><span class="accent">The approach is ours.</span></h2><p>These independent publications document challenges in process representation, multidimensional analysis, adoption and AI. They do not evaluate or endorse RE-ViVE; our architecture and deployment results are the evidence for our approach.</p></div><ul class="research"><li><a href="https://vdaalst.com/publications/p1101.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">W. M. P. van der Aalst — A Practitioner's Guide to Process Mining: Limitations of the Directly-Follows Graph ↗</a><small>Misleading loops, filtering and performance interpretation in directly-follows representations.</small></li><li><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10115-026-02904-0" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Advancing Object-Centric Process Mining with Multi-Dimensional Data Operations (2026) ↗</a><small>Analytical granularity and multidimensional operations in object-centric process mining.</small></li><li><a href="https://aisel.aisnet.org/ecis2026/bpm/bpm/2/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Challenges and Opportunities of Object-Centric Process Mining in Industry: A Research Agenda (2026) ↗</a><small>Technical and adoption challenges seen in industry.</small></li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.00116" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">No AI Without PI! Object-Centric Process Mining as the Enabler for Generative, Predictive, and Prescriptive AI (2025) ↗</a><small>Why process intelligence underpins enterprise AI.</small></li></ul></div></section>
